# The Silent Morning View and the 20-Minute Walk  
## 6 – 8 March 2026

It was early this past week when, upon waking up in the morning, I consciously took a moment to look out of my dormitory window without the thought of anything but what lie outside. As I observed the bare elm branches underneath the blueish grey sky of intensifying twilight, a sense of almost incommunicable serenity came upon me. The faint idea of being able to wake to the same sight in a world now in the far past emerged. I normally associate this tranquillity with full immersion in nature, where there exists state of both unwavering timelessness and temporality that is independent from modern life. Yet, after making it a habit to observe this silent view of Harvard Yard for just 1 minute each morning, a sense of restorative bliss has not yet failed to begin each day.

It is this clearing of the mind and focused observation that reflects a more general theme found among other lessons that I have written about. These small revelations or changes in habits all foster a certain serenity that is easily lost in the excesses of modern life. This is especially true in my case, where a perceived deficit of time to complete college work has accentuated a bad habit of thinking about the next tasks of the day before the task at hand is finished. I will not deviate into this phenomenon—one that has been longstanding, and that I only recently internalised enough to address. Nevertheless, I have found that habits like this may often be amplified by modern communications technology. They may also be just as easily corrigible. One may choose to live in a manner that is reflective of an older, more natural state of life that is easy to avoid in modern society. Returning to the newfound moment I now take every morning, this is not to say that one cannot immerse himself in modern labours. In fact, this moment of silence brings forth a vivacity my studies and actually highlights the fulfilment of such labours.

I must mention another recent finding. This small discovery of mine has also shown what good can come from taking the time to simply observe something easy to miss in 21st century American life. Although I have been gifted to run on weekday afternoons and to visit local hiking trails on weekends throughout the semesters, practically all of these activities bring me westward. That is, along the artery that is the Charles River Reservation or in the same general direction on bike paths further north—all stretching away from the city. But for all of the importance that I place upon gaining bearings of the land as a means to ground oneself in it, there exist areas of Cambridge less than 800 metres to the north or to the east of my dormitory that I have yet to visit. 

This phenomenon prefaces a new habit of mine: the 20-minute walk. Although the time required to bike to most trails from Harvard Yard makes necessitates allocating at least 1½ hours to the entire exchange of getting to walk in a natural area proper, there are occasional weekend days when this is infeasible. As I search for employment next academic year, it is likely that what I have discovered two Sundays ago will only be of greater use; it is remarkable how wholesome for the mind and the body a walk as short as 15 or 20 minutes can be. As obvious as this fact may seem to the reader, it is easy to not recognise these opportunities during shorter windows of time—especially if one does not associate urban areas with much of the beauty that can be experienced while walking. I will end with a finding on my walk last weekend that illustrates this point well. A small quadrangle within Harvard Yard remained unobserved by me until that day. This area contains Mower Hall, a pleasantly small building whose backside I had only ever taken passing glances of from the road. Without the newfound 20-minute walk, I would have never enjoyed the sight of the pale grass and ivy emerging from the melting snow in the spaces between the buildings and the Harvard Yard fence. One of these contains a small sundial on a pillar, whereon it is inscribed, “ON THIS MONUMENT RESTS ETERNITY.”

## An Addendum
There remains another way to foster a needed awareness of the land that, as I realized in earnest this morning, I too have been neglecting. Adequately familiarizing oneself with the history of where one lives is an arguable responsibility if the means to do so are available. My childhood home rests on land that—with the exception of an Indian trail on an adjacent ridge—has a traceable history that commences with settlers from an already-established United States. Information about the peoples who had before lived and died in the immediately surrounding areas is relatively scarce.

Beyond my passing interest in the first English settlements in the New England, however, it came upon me today how my knowledge about the early history of where I now live is ashamedly shallow. I was reminded of a work that conveys the aspirations of the first English settlers. “A Modell of Christian Charity,” most commonly attributed to John Winthrop, contains the famous designation of the Massachusetts Bay Colony as the future “City on a Hill.” The historical and moral implications of this writing or its author are beyond the scope of this piece, but that only attests to a needed minimum level of familiarity with the first settlers of one’s home area. This naturally involves the peoples who have inhabited these lands for millennia, and records about these coastal peoples from the earliest European settlers are abundant. Two resolutions of mine have resulted. I plan to read “A Modell…” in its entirety, and I plan to address a pre-existing desire to better familiarize myself with what native peoples lived in what is now Cambridge along with the places that I visit often throughout the semester. Perhaps most importantly—as I touched upon in my reflections around the importance of the hearth and the granary this winter—this should also include how these people lived.

### Postscript
Although I rejoice in the fact that I will now be waking well before sunrise due to daylight saving time, I no longer will immediately look out to the twilight. I will continue to take this minute to observe after getting up despite the low visibility outside. Furthermore, when the sun does rise, it is likely that taking another minute to enjoy the view in the midst of studying can be just as refreshing.
